               <p xml:id="idm112">In 1838 Hans Christian Andersen adapted the Austrian author Ferdinand Raimund's play "Der Verschwender" of 1834. Johannes Frederik Frøhlich and Ivar Bredal had agreed to compose the songs, Hartmann the melodramas and August Bournonville to make the choreography. However, less than a week before the premiere it was cancelled; instead Andersen handed in the manuscript to the director of The Royal Theatre, but the censor, Chr. K.F. Molbech, rejected it. Raimund's play was not performed until 1849, when the text had been translated by F.L. Liebenberg, adapted by Johan Christian Hostrup and set to music by Hartmann.</p>
            </history>
